PROTOCOL
26: Gradeable answer record: `case_id`, `status`, `claims[{claim_id, answer, conditions, citations[]}]`, `limitations`. Citations contain original SHA256, repository-relative path, archive/member chain when applicable, and a native object/property locator or exact XML/key/byte location appropriate to the claim.
27: For diagrams, record owning VI, nested structure/case, object/terminal/wire identity and native view region; attach a hashed native capture and object map when identifiers are unstable. A screenshot or exported node ID alone is insufficient: the reviewer must resolve the claim back into the preserved original.
28: Before reader exposure, freeze an independently checked reference inventory per task: stable required-claim IDs, atomic facts/relations, conditions, acceptable alternatives, numerical tolerances, original/native reference locators and hashes, and disallowed inferences. Identify separately the required answer claims and required reader observations; neither inventory may be inferred from submitted answers or exports. Record inventory/key revisions and unresolved references. Development keys may be public; withheld keys, case details and revealing evidence must never enter this repository or its history.
29: Account for every required ID and every submitted factual assertion, including implicit assertions in prose and limitations. Preserve the submitted text and its mapping to reference IDs; split compound assertions into atomic claims under frozen rules. Repeated equivalent assertions earn credit once; omissions retain their required slots. An assertion and its negation are a contradiction even when one matches the key: mark the affected item incorrect and retain both assertions. Unmatched assertions enter a separate extra-claim ledger and require independent checking; true extras cannot replace omissions, and false or unverified extras cannot disappear from scoring. Freeze these mapping/adjudication rules before runs and retain scorer/checker decisions.
30: Score reading fidelity, answer correctness and source grounding separately using the following item rules; faithful extraction alone does not establish correct reasoning, and answer correctness cannot establish fidelity.
31: 
32: | Dimension | Required unit and correct outcome | Other item outcomes |
33: |---|---|---|
34: | Reading fidelity | Each fixed required reader observation matches its independently checked native reference, including required types, properties and relations under declared tolerances. | Partially represented compound content is partial; a conflicting value/relation is incorrect; absent content is missing. Unsupported access, inaccessible diagrams, tool errors and unverified native references retain their explicit outcomes and never earn correct credit. |
35: | Answer correctness | Each required answer claim matches an independently established expected claim under its conditions and tolerances. | Incomplete content is partial; false claims or contradictions are incorrect; omitted claims are missing; claims without sufficient independent evidence are unverified. An unsupported-case declaration remains unsupported, not correct. |
36: | Source grounding | Each required answer claim has resolvable original-artifact citations supporting its complete asserted content and conditions. | Support for only part is partial; a wrong or non-supporting citation is incorrect; absent claims/citations are missing; inaccessible evidence is unverified. A correct answer with a wrong citation is incorrect in grounding independently of correctness. |
37: 
38: Reader-observation contract: before exposure, an independent preparer freezes probe IDs, artifact/member hashes, reader entry points and arguments, observation scope, required observation IDs and native-reference alignment rules. Choose probes from the task/native inventories, independently of agent answers and reader outputs; include the types, objects, wires, properties and relationships needed by the task. Pin ordering, limits, any adaptive branches and their selection rules; record unprobed scope as missing coverage. No favorable agent-selected calls may substitute for required probes.
39: For every probe, preserve the exact request, reader/configuration revision, environment, raw response bytes or files and hashes, timestamps, exit/error records and any truncation. Preserve empty responses and failed calls. A separate alignment record maps raw-output locations to required native-reference IDs and records absent, extra, duplicate and contradictory assertions; normalization must retain raw evidence and cannot invent missing facts. Unsupported operations, missing dependencies/licenses/environment, protected/removed diagrams, empty or truncated required content, errors and unverified references never count as matching observations. A successful call or valid wrapper alone earns no fidelity credit.
40: Freeze denominators per task and dimension: all required answer IDs for correctness and grounding, and all required observation IDs for fidelity. Report counts in every item-outcome category over those fixed totals, plus separate extra-claim counts by outcome and contradiction/duplicate counts; do not enlarge denominators with submitted claims or remove unavailable items. Report all planned cases as the coverage denominator alongside attempted/executed/scored counts. Any conditional summary must also show its eligible denominator and excluded/unavailable counts; no single aggregate merges the three dimensions.
41: Apply the following case-assessment rules in order, separately for each dimension. An absent or empty required reference inventory is unverified, never vacuously correct. With an established inventory, any incorrect required item or extra assertion, including a false assertion, contradiction or non-supporting citation in grounding, makes that dimension incorrect. Otherwise, any unverified required item or extra makes it unverified. Otherwise, every required item and extra must be correct (fully supported for grounding) for a correct assessment. Of the remaining cases, any correct/partial required item or extra makes the assessment partial; otherwise any tool error makes it error; otherwise all required items unsupported makes it unsupported; all other combinations of missing/unsupported items make it missing. Keep operational limitations and their affected IDs alongside this assessment; partial, missing, unsupported, unverified and error outcomes never become full passes. These are accounting rules, not owner quality thresholds.
55: | Typedef and diagram changes | Preserve both original revisions; apply a documented edit to derived copies, inspect affected consumers/native differences and run discriminating controlled inputs. | Before/after hashes, edit recipe, affected/unaffected checks, native difference evidence and observed behavior. |
56: | Projects, packages and builds | Inspect native project/package properties and original declarations; explicitly check resolution and, where authorized, installation/build behavior in an isolated job. | Declared versus resolved dependency lists, member identities, build settings/results and unresolved items. |
57: | Targets, hardware and compiled artifacts | Inspect available native interfaces and run only with the required runtime, target and hardware or declared simulator. | Actual execution environment and observed scope; simulation or structural inspection cannot certify hardware behavior or recover removed diagrams. |
58: 
59: Documentation supports semantic interpretation only when pinned by version, section/location and content hash or preserved permitted excerpt; it does not establish what a particular original diagram contains. Unavailable documentation remains a gap.
60: An independent checker must resolve each expected claim and citation against original/native evidence without using the reader's output to derive the answer. Record case/key revision, evidence hashes, checker identity/role and session, procedure, claim-level agreement/disagreement and unresolved uncertainty. Disagreement blocks that claim's use as an established key until adjudicated and rechecked.
61: Use more than one observable when necessary to distinguish plausible wrong answers: selected inputs must separate intended computation from known alternatives, and native structural evidence must support dependencies not distinguishable by outputs alone. Record any underdetermined behavior explicitly.
62: Predeclare numeric precision, units, comparison rules, nondeterministic behavior and allowed variation per case. Missing runtime, dependencies, licenses, native diagram access or independent check yields an unavailable/unverified key and an unexecuted or limited case, never a passing semantic result.
97: |---|---|---|
98: | Wrong answer | Start from a checked correct response; change a substantive computation, dependency or typedef-impact claim while retaining its valid source locator. Use controlled inputs/native evidence that distinguish the replacement claim. | Correctness detects the false claim; citation resolution alone cannot make it correct. Retain the true and false claims and evidence explaining the difference. |
99: | Wrong citation | Keep a correct answer but cite a different existing object, case branch, member or revision that does not support it; also test an unresolvable locator. | Grounding rejects the unsupported citation independently of answer correctness. Checking only that the file exists or has a valid hash must fail this challenge. |
100: | Selective omission | Remove one required answer claim and, separately, one required reader observation from otherwise checked complete submissions containing at least two required IDs in the affected dimension; retain the remaining correct content and citations. | The omitted ID remains missing in its frozen denominator; correctness/grounding or fidelity respectively becomes partial, never complete. True extra claims cannot replace the omitted ID. |
README
1: # Writing method extension
2: 
3: Design only, unreviewed. No artifact has been edited, no agent comparison or LabVIEW job has run, and no native key or benchmark readiness is established. [method.json](method.json) extends the [existing protocol](../protocol.md), [typedef-impact](../change-cases/typedef-impact.spec.json) and [diagram-difference](../change-cases/diagram-difference.spec.json) specifications. The [evaluation contract](../../docs/perpetua/evaluation.md), [role sequence](../../docs/perpetua/roles.md) and [writing goal](../../goal/goal.md#pisanie) remain authoritative. Historical evidence, preparation index and milestone status retain their identities; this integration extends only the named writing-method files.
4: 
5: The [bounded unsuccessful POC](../../docs/research/poc/delivery.md) supplied no executable semantic edit, changed VI, save-version demonstration or runtime check. It is evidence of those limits, never an editor implementation or oracle. Parsing, changed bytes, a successful process, matching selected outputs alone, and owner silence cannot establish writing success.
6: 
7: ## Existing obligations and additions
8: 
9: `existing_requirements` E1–E8 records exactly what is inherited, with source lines/anchors: task/provenance records; independent keys and original preservation; derived-copy change recipes; separate answer/grounding/reading measurements; public/private lineage boundaries; paired baselines; positive/rejecting controls; resource and acceptance sequence. `new_requirements` W1–W8 maps each addition back to those obligations: actual artifact outputs, absent creation destinations, frozen semantic/preservation IDs, preserved save versions, separate artifact/native judgments, compiled source/rebuild chains, writing output accounting and artifact-level rejection controls. This extension supplies no replacement protocol or executable scoring policy.
10: 
11: ## Task contracts
12: 
13: | Task | Requested artifact result | Independent expected result and current gap |
14: |---|---|---|
15: | WC1 | Create `Increment.vi` with I32 `x + 1 -> y`, target save version `21008000`, no state or extra operations | Exact arithmetic vectors and graph obligation derive from the request. Native reference, connector map, documentation pins and separate checking are unavailable. |
16: | WM1 | Insert one Boolean NOT immediately before the selected output in the original pinned VI from DD-E1 | False/true inversion is specified; original computation, native locators, save version and controlled reference results remain unavailable. DD-E2 is a separate presentation preservation control, not a behavior edit. |
17: | WM2 | Wrap the pinned typedef root as `original_value` plus Boolean `impact_probe=false`, retaining defaults and typedef mode | Requested cluster semantics are specified. Confirmed consumers, propagation save set, save versions and effects remain unavailable; broken consumers must be reported, never repaired silently. |
18: | WB1 | Change matched source increment 1 to 2 and rebuild a packed library, retaining exported API and source versions | Exact arithmetic and API obligations are specified. Matching source/build originals, toolchain and native evidence are absent. Source-only work and unexecuted build remain separate; binary patching is excluded. |
19: 
20: Every contract records source/requirement anchors, preconditions, output paths, original hashes where available, semantic IDs, unrelated-content/version invariants, answer IDs and independent oracle provenance requirements. Requirement-derived values are independent of candidate output; they are not claimed as observed LabVIEW results. Before exposure, a separate preparer/checker must freeze original/native object maps, dependency closure, typed fixtures, resets, bounded timeouts, exact comparisons, evidence hashes and per-ID agreement. Unknown native locators or versions block native/full-trial eligibility; WP1 text-only preparation uses the exact pinned XML/byte locator while those prerequisites remain pending; they cannot be filled from the evaluated agent's output. Native disagreement blocks the affected key.
21: 
22: Artifact fidelity checks the complete output file set, typed objects and relations, intended delta, unrelated objects/files and per-file save version. Correct prose does not rescue a wrong artifact. Answer correctness and original/output source support follow the existing required-ID, extra-claim and contradiction rules separately. Native load, compile/build, controlled execution and hardware/target execution each require their own actual evidence; editing without LabVIEW can produce an artifact while all native outcomes remain unexecuted. No blanket exception permits conversion or unrelated resave changes.
23: 
24: ## Paired writing comparison
25: 
26: Reuse the [runner contract](../baseline/runner-contract.md) and [precision plan](../precision/README.md). Freeze a hashed writing addendum to the existing manifest: identical requests, original copies, allowed documentation/images, common prompts, ordinary editing facilities, model/settings and total token/context/output/artifact-byte/wall-time budgets. Only evaluated-tool access differs; its output/time/cost counts against the shared totals. Neither arm receives LabVIEW access in this stratum. Any later native validation is a separate trusted job after both outputs are frozen.
27: 
28: Each repetition uses fresh sessions and dependency-isolated workspaces, deterministic randomized arm order and frozen keys. Four development pilot repetitions is the existing design allocation, conditional on quota; evaluation repetitions remain undecided. Retain every planned slot, failed call, retry, absent/invalid output, actual artifact bytes/hash and unexecuted attempt. Report artifact semantic and preservation results, answers, grounding and native results separately, with fixed denominators and planned/attempted/produced/native-executed/scored/complete-pair counts. The inherited paired lineage-cluster uncertainty procedure and missing-pair bounds extend to separate artifact semantic and preservation fractions; no owner threshold or completed comparison is implied.
29: 
30: Public originals, fixtures and derivatives stay in development lineage groups. None is withheld or demonstrably model-unseen. Future restricted writing cases and keys require commissioned sources, a custodian, actual allocation, tested runner isolation and independent review; store readiness alone supplies none of those. `coverage` lists uncovered families and versions explicitly. All writing runtime counts are zero; 2024 is unverified and no fallback or family reduction is selected.
31: 
32: ## Reproducible design check
33: 
34: Run from the repository root:
35: 
36: ```text
37: python3 -I benchmark/writing/verify_method.py
38: ```
39: 
40: The stdlib checker reads repository-local method, controls, pinned original/license, historical evidence and the new integration provenance, accepts no external input and writes no files. Neither `LABVIEW_ENVIRONMENT` nor `.session/` is needed. It checks fixed task/requirement inventories, required fields, arithmetic/inversion consistency and dimension/coverage declarations, then checks hand-authored fictional graph snapshots against separately stated requirements. The project-text stratum uses standard-library XML parsing and exact byte comparison. It provides no native LabVIEW reader, writer, benchmark runner or production evaluator.
41: 
42: Positive controls retain creation, modification, source-only modification with unavailable rebuild, and a matched but unexecuted pair. A valid fictional `x+1` graph passes its own request but fails the `x+2` request, exposing a constant writer. Forty-four in-memory mutations must reject for exact asserted reasons: the original 35 controls for no-op/changed-bytes-only, wrong graph/type, unintended edits/files, missing output, version drift/unknown version, unsupported artifact, wrong answers/citations/extras, invented native success, overwritten originals, creation overwrite, mismatched pair inputs/budgets, missing arm/accounting, method overclaims and missing fields, missing compiled source, invented rebuild and mismatched source/build linkage; plus nine controls correcting review #485. Two preserve originals at `SYNTHETIC-v0` while outputs and frozen expectations remain at `SYNTHETIC-v1`; ordinary and source-rebuild modifications must both reject for `version-drift`. Modification checks original-to-output version equality as well as the frozen version; creation uses its requested version. Seven controls independently delete each mandatory pair setting from the frozen dictionary and both arms, requiring `pair-setting-required`. The mandatory names (`request`, `originals`, `allowed_inputs`, `model`, `budgets`, `key_revision`, `repetitions`) map the documented paired obligations in protocol lines 79–84 and the runner contract to this bounded synthetic representation; equality among incomplete dictionaries cannot satisfy them. Matching values, matching key sets and paired-arm invariants remain checked. The fixed control inventory prevents silently dropping a rejecting test. This is a bounded contract check, not a complete semantic checker; coordinated edits to fixtures and checker still need independent review.
43: 
44: [verification.json](verification.json) records the actual check and input hashes; [input-pins.json](input-pins.json) records baseline identity and preserved evidence hashes. [review-corrections.json](review-corrections.json) pins review #485, the starting/reviewed identities and six hashes, protected-byte checks, and exact before/after counterexample mutations and reason codes. Passing means design consistency only. Synthetic graph fields are not decoded LabVIEW objects, `SYNTHETIC-v1` is not a real save version, and none of these controls produces native evidence or authorizes measurement.
45: 
46: ## Next review and decisions
47: 
48: Give a separate reviewer the parent's pinned candidate commit/tree, baseline `486030b461e57ca0f322a17a5b7853e83e9655e3` / tree `cf75cf6892de2f9410fa09ee2589dcba47d825a4`, output hashes and original-source evidence. Review requirement coverage, expected-result independence, control discrimination and all unavailable outcomes. This task does not seek method acceptance. Later owner/resource decisions remain native authoring/validation capability and grants (#169), matching sources/build tools, independently authored/checked fixtures, restricted allocation and run isolation, model quota/precision targets, and all-version writing effort/compatibility estimates before any fallback. Only after separate review can the owner consider method acceptance before product writing implementation. Executable benchmark scoring belongs to a Policymaker-ordered evaluator change and its separate verifier.
